00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Additional funding provided by

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
When we ended last time, we were

4
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,000
discussing Locke's idea of government by consent.

5
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,500
And the question arose, what are the limits on

6
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:49,000
government that even the agreement of the majority can't override?

7
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
That was the question we ended with.

8
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
We saw in the case of property rights,

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,000
that on Locke's view,

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
a democratically elected government has

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
the right to tax people.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
It has to be taxation with consent, because it does

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
involve the taking of people's property for the common good.

14
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,500
But it doesn't require the consent of each individual

15
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:28,000
at the time the tax is enacted or collected.

16
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,000
So, we're going to look at the tax.

17
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,000
What it does require

18
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,000
is a prior act of consent to join the society,

19
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
to take on the political obligation.

20
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
But once you take on that obligation,

21
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
you agree to be bound by the majority.

22
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
So much for taxation.

23
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
But what you may ask about the right to life,

24
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,500
can the government conscript people

25
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:03,000
and send them into battle?

26
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
What about the idea that we own ourselves?

27
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Isn't the idea of self-possession violated?

28
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
If the government can, through

29
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
coercive legislation and enforcement powers,

30
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
say, you must go risk

31
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
your life to fight in Iraq,

32
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,000
what would Locke say? Does the

33
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,000
government have the right to do that?

34
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Yes.

35
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
In fact, he says in 139,

36
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
he says,

37
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
what matters is that the political authority,

38
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
or the military authority,

39
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,000
not be arbitrary. That's what matters.

40
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
And he gives a wonderful example. He says,

41
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
a sergeant, even a sergeant,

42
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
let alone a general, a sergeant can command a soldier

43
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
to go right up to the face of the cannon,

44
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
where he is almost sure to die.

45
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
That the sergeant can do.

46
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
The general can condemn the soldier

47
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
to death for deserting his post,

48
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
or for not obeying even a desperate order.

49
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
But with all their power over life and death,

50
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
what these officers can't do

51
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:35,000
is take a penny of that soldier's money.

52
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Because that has nothing to do with the rightful authority.

53
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
That would be arbitrary.

54
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,000
And it would be corrupt.

55
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:46,000
So, consent winds up being very powerful in Locke.

56
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,500
Not consent of the individual to

57
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:51,000
the particular tax or military order,

58
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
but consent to join the government

59
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:54,500
and to be bound by

60
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:56,000
the majority in the first place.

61
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
That's the consent that matters.

62
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:03,000
And it matters so powerfully

63
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,500
that even the limited government created

64
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:08,000
by the fact that we have

65
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
an unalienable right to life, liberty, and property,

66
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
even that limited government is only limited in the sense

67
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
that it has to govern by generally applicable laws,

68
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
the rule of law, it can't be arbitrary.

69
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:22,000
That's Locke.

70
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Well, this raises a question about consent.

71
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Why is consent such a powerful moral instrument

72
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
in creating political authority and the obligation to obey?

73
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Today we begin to investigate the question of consent

74
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
by looking at a concrete case,

75
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
the case of military conscription.

76
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Now, some people say,

77
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
if we have a fundamental right

78
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:03,000
that arises from the idea that we own ourselves,

79
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
it's a violation of that right

80
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
for government to conscript citizens to go fight in wars.

81
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Others disagree.

82
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Others say that's a legitimate power of government,

83
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
of democratically elected governments.

84
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
But that's not the case, anyhow,

85
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,000
and that we have an obligation to obey.

86
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Let's take the case of the

87
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
United States fighting a war in Iraq.

88
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:41,000
News accounts tell us that the military

89
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
is having great difficulty meeting its recruitment targets.

90
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Consider three policies that the U.S. government,

91
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,000
U.S. government might undertake

92
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,500
to deal with the fact that

93
00:05:56,500 --> 00:06:00,000
it's not achieving its recruiting targets.

94
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Solution number one,

95
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
increase the pay and benefits

96
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
to attract a sufficient number of soldiers.

97
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Option number two,

98
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:18,000
shift to a system of military conscription.

99
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Have a lottery,

100
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,000
and whosoever numbers are drawn,

101
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,000
go to fight in Iraq.

102
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
System number three,

103
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
outsource.

104
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:41,000
Hire what traditionally have been called mercenaries,

105
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
people around the world who are qualified,

106
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:45,000
able to do the work,

107
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:46,000
able to fight well,

108
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
and who are willing to do it

109
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
for the existing wage.

110
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
So let's take a quick poll here.

111
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:01,000
How many favor increasing the pay?

112
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Huge majority.

113
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:07,000
How many favor going to conscription?

114
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
One, two.

115
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
All right, maybe a dozen people in the room

116
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
favor conscription.

117
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
What about the outsourcing solution?

118
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Okay, so there may be,

119
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:28,000
what, two, three dozen.

120
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
During the Civil War,

121
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:38,000
the Union used a combination

122
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:42,000
of conscription and the market system

123
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
to fill the ranks of the military

124
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
to fight in the Civil War.

125
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,000
It was a system that began with conscription,

126
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:56,000
but if you were drafted

127
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,000
and didn't want to serve,

128
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,000
you could hire a substitute to take your place.

129
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:06,000
And many people did.

130
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:11,000
You could pay whatever the market required

131
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:14,000
in order to find a substitute.

132
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
People ran ads in newspapers.

133
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:19,000
In the classified ads.

134
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Offering $500,

135
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:26,000
sometimes $1,000,

136
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
for a substitute who would go fight the Civil War

137
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
in their place.

138
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
In fact, it's reported that Andrew Carnegie

139
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
was drafted

140
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,000
and hired a substitute to take his place

141
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
for an amount

142
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
that was a little less

143
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
than the amount he spent in a year

144
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
on fancy cigars.

145
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:54,000
Now, I want to get your views

146
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
about the Civil War system.

147
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
Call it the hybrid system.

148
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
Conscription, but with a buyout provision.

149
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,000
How many think it was a just system?

150
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,000
How many would defend the Civil War system?

151
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Anybody?

152
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
One? Anybody else?

153
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:14,000
Two? Three?

154
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
Four? Five?

155
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,000
How many think it was unjust?

156
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
Most of you don't like the Civil War system.

157
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,000
You think it's unjust.

158
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
Let's hear an objection.

159
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:28,000
Why don't you like it?

160
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:34,000
What's wrong with it?

161
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
Yes?

162
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:40,000
Well, by paying $300 for,

163
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
to be exempt one time around,

164
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
you're really putting a price on the,

165
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,000
on valuing human life.

166
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:47,000
And we established earlier

167
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
that's really hard to do.

168
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
So, they're trying to accomplish something

169
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
that really isn't feasible.

170
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:54,000
Good.

171
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:59,000
So, paying someone $300 or $500 or $1,000.

172
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:00,000
You're basically saying

173
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
that's what their life is worth to you.

174
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:03,000
That's what their life is worth.

175
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
It's putting a dollar value on life.

176
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:06,000
That's good.

177
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:07,000
And what's your name?

178
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:08,000
Liz.

179
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
Liz.

180
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
Well, who has an answer for Liz?

181
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
You defended the Civil War system.

182
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:16,000
What do you say?

183
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,000
If you don't like the price,

184
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
then you have the freedom

185
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
to not be sold or hire.

186
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
So, it's completely up to you.

187
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
And I don't think it's necessarily putting

188
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
a specific price on you.

189
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:32,000
And if it's done by himself,

190
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
I don't think there's anything necessarily

191
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:35,000
morally wrong with that.

192
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:41,000
So, the person who takes the $500, let's say,

193
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:42,000
he's putting

194
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
his own price on his life

195
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
or on the risk of his life.

196
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
And he should have the freedom

197
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:51,000
to choose to do that.

198
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:52,000
Exactly.

199
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:53,000
What's your name?

200
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:54,000
Jason.

201
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
Jason. Thank you.

202
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
Now, we need to hear from another critic

203
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
of the Civil War system.

204
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Yes.

205
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
It's a kind of coercion almost

206
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
to people who have lower incomes.

207
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
For Carnegie, he can totally ignore the draft.

208
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:12,000
$300 is irrelevant in terms of the cost.

209
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:13,000
It's not something that's

210
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:14,000
in the hands of his income.

211
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:15,000
For someone of a lower income,

212
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
they're essentially being coerced to draft,

213
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:19,000
to be drafted.

214
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
Or, I mean, it's probably they're not able

215
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:23,000
to find a replacement.

216
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:24,000
Tell me your name.

217
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:25,000
Sam.

218
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:26,000
Sam.

219
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:27,000
All right.

220
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:28,000
So, you say, Sam,

221
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:33,000
that when a poor laborer buys his,

222
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:37,000
accepts $300 to fight in the Civil War,

223
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:42,000
he is in effect being coerced by that money,

224
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
his economic circumstances.

225
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
Whereas Carnegie can go off,

226
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:47,000
pay the money,

227
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
and not serve.

228
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:51,000
All right.

229
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
I want to hear if someone has a reply

230
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,000
to Sam's argument

231
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
that what looks like a free exchange

232
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:04,000
is actually coercive.

233
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:07,000
Who has an answer to Sam?

234
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:08,000
Go ahead.

235
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
I'd actually agree with him in saying that.

236
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:12,000
You agree with Sam?

237
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:15,000
I agree with him in saying that it is coercion

238
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
in the sense that it robs the individual

239
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
of his ability to reason.

240
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:21,000
Okay.

241
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:22,000
And what's your name?

242
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:23,000
Raul.

243
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:24,000
All right.

244
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
So, Raul and Sam agree

245
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
that what looks like a free exchange,

246
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
free choice, voluntary act,

247
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:34,000
is actually, it involves coercion.

248
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
It's profound coercion of the worst kind

249
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
because it falls so disproportionately

250
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
upon one segment of the society.

251
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:42,000
Good.

252
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,000
So, Raul and Sam have made a powerful point.

253
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:48,000
Who would like to reply?

254
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
Who has an answer for Sam and Raul?

255
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
Go ahead.

256
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
I just, I don't think that these drafting systems

257
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
are really terribly different from,

258
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:01,000
you know, all volunteer army sort of recruiting strategies.

259
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
The whole idea of, you know, having benefits

260
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
and pay for joining the army is, you know,

261
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,000
sort of a coercive strategy to get people to join.

262
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:11,000
It is true that, you know,

263
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:12,500
it is true that

264
00:13:12,500 --> 00:13:14,000
military volunteers come from disproportionately,

265
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:18,000
you know, lower economic status and also, you know,

266
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
from certain regions of the country where you can use,

267
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
like, the patriotism to try and coerce people

268
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:24,000
to feel like it's the right thing to do,

269
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
to volunteer and go over to Iraq.

270
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:27,000
And tell me your name.

271
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Emily.

272
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:29,000
All right.

273
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Emily says, and Raul,

274
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:34,500
you're going to have to

275
00:13:34,500 --> 00:13:36,000
reply to this, so get ready.

276
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Emily says, fair enough.

277
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
There is a coercive element to the Civil War system

278
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:46,000
when the laborer takes the

279
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:49,000
place of Andrew Carnegie for $500.

280
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
Emily concedes that.

281
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,500
But she says, if that troubles

282
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:56,000
you about the Civil War system,

283
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,500
shouldn't that also trouble you

284
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:03,000
about the volunteer army today?

285
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,000
And let me, and before you answer,

286
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
how did you vote on the first poll?

287
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:09,000
Did you defend the volunteer army?

288
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
I didn't vote.

289
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:12,000
You didn't vote.

290
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:13,000
No.

291
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
By the way, you didn't vote.

292
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:16,500
Did you sell your vote to

293
00:14:16,500 --> 00:14:18,000
the person sitting next to you?

294
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:19,000
No.

295
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
All right.

296
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,000
So what would you say to that argument?

297
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
I think that the circumstances are different

298
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:32,000
in that there was conscription in the Civil War.

299
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
There is no draft today.

300
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:37,000
And I think that the volunteers,

301
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:41,000
and I think that the volunteers for the army today

302
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
have a more profound sense of patriotism

303
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
that is of an individual choice

304
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,500
than those who were forced into

305
00:14:47,500 --> 00:14:50,000
the military in the Civil War.

306
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
Somehow less coerced.

307
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Less coerced.

308
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
Even though there is still inequality in American society.

309
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,000
Even though, as Emily points out,

310
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
the makeup of the American military is

311
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
not reflective of the population as a whole.

312
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
Let's just do an experiment here.

313
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:12,000
How many here have either served in the military

314
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:13,500
or have a family member

315
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:15,000
who has served in the military?

316
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:18,000
In this generation, not parents.

317
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:19,000
Family members.

318
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
In this generation.

319
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:24,000
And how many have neither served

320
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:31,000
nor have any brothers or sisters who have served?

321
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:34,000
Does that bear out your point, Emily?

322
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:35,000
Yes.

323
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:36,000
All right.

324
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Now we need to hear from...

325
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:47,000
Most of you defended the idea of the all-volunteer military.

326
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:50,000
Overwhelmingly.

327
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:53,500
And yet, overwhelmingly, people considered

328
00:15:53,500 --> 00:15:57,000
the Civil War system unjust.

329
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,500
Sam and Raoul articulated reasons for

330
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:04,000
objecting to the Civil War system.

331
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,000
It took place against a background of inequality,

332
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,500
and therefore the choices people made

333
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:12,000
to buy their way into military service

334
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:16,000
were not truly free, but at least partly coerced.

335
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:19,000
Then, Emily extends that argument

336
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:22,000
in the form of a challenge.

337
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:23,000
All right.

338
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:28,000
Everyone here who voted in favor of the all-volunteer army

339
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:31,000
should be able...

340
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
should have to explain, well,

341
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
what's the difference in principle?

342
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
Doesn't the all-volunteer army

343
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:41,000
simply universalize the feature

344
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,500
that almost everyone found objectionable

345
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:46,000
in the Civil War buyout provision?

346
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:49,000
Did I state the challenge fairly, Emily?

347
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:50,000
Yes.

348
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:51,000
Okay.

349
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
So we need to hear from

350
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
a defender of the all-volunteer military

351
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:58,000
who can address Emily's challenge.

352
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,000
Who can do that?

353
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Go ahead.

354
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
The difference between the Civil War system

355
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:05,500
and the all-volunteer army system

356
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:07,000
is that in the Civil War,

357
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
you're being hired not by

358
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
the government but by an individual.

359
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:12,500
And as a result, different people

360
00:17:12,500 --> 00:17:14,000
who get hired by different individuals

361
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
get paid different amounts.

362
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
In the case of the all-volunteer army,

363
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
everyone who gets hired is hired by the government

364
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
and gets paid the same amount.

365
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,500
It's precisely the universalization

366
00:17:24,500 --> 00:17:27,000
of essentially paying your service,

367
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
of paying your way into the

368
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
army that makes the all-volunteer army just...

369
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
Emily?

370
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,500
I guess I'd frame

371
00:17:36,500 --> 00:17:39,000
the principle difference slightly differently.

372
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,500
On the all-volunteer army, it's possible

373
00:17:41,500 --> 00:17:44,000
for somebody to just step aside

374
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
and not really think about the war at all.

375
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,000
It's possible to say, well, I don't need the money.

376
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
I don't need to have an opinion about this.

377
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
I don't need to feel obligated to

378
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
take my part and defend my country.

379
00:17:55,000 --> 00:18:01,000
With an explicit draft, then there's the threat at least

380
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:02,500
that every individual will have

381
00:18:02,500 --> 00:18:04,000
to make some sort of decision

382
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
regarding military conscription.

383
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
And perhaps in that way, it's more equitable.

384
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
It's true that Andrew Carnegie

385
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
might not serve in any case,

386
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:16,000
but in one, he can completely step aside from it.

387
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:19,000
In the other, there's some level of responsibility.

388
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
While you're there, Emily, so what system do you favor?

389
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
Conscription?

390
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:27,000
I would be hard-pressed to say, but I think so,

391
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:28,500
because it makes the whole

392
00:18:28,500 --> 00:18:30,000
country feel a sense of responsibility

393
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
for the conflict instead of having

394
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
a war that's maybe ideologically supported

395
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:38,000
by a few, but only if there's no real responsibility.

396
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:42,000
Good. Who wants to reply? Go ahead.

397
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:45,000
So I was going to say that the fundamental difference

398
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
between the all-volunteer army and then

399
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
the army in the Civil War

400
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
is that in the all-volunteer

401
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
army, if you want to volunteer,

402
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:57,000
that comes first, and then the pay comes after,

403
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
whereas in the Civil War system,

404
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:00,000
it's the same.

405
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
The people who are accepting

406
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
the pay aren't necessarily doing it

407
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
because they want to.

408
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
They're just doing it for the money first.

409
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:12,000
What motivation beyond the pay do you think is operating

410
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:15,000
in the case of the all-volunteer army?

411
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
Like patriotism for the country?

412
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,000
Patriotism. Well, what about...

413
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:23,000
And a desire to defend the country.

414
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
I mean, there is some motivation in pay,

415
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:29,000
but the fact that it's first and foremost

416
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
an all-volunteer army will

417
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
motivate them, I think, personally.

418
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
Do you think it's better... And tell me your name.

419
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:36,000
Jackie.

420
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:37,500
Jackie, do you think it's better

421
00:19:37,500 --> 00:19:39,000
if people serve in the military

422
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:40,500
out of a sense of

423
00:19:40,500 --> 00:19:42,000
patriotism than just for the money?

424
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:45,000
Yes, definitely, because the people who...

425
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:46,000
That was one of the main

426
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:47,000
problems in the Civil War army

427
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:48,500
is that the people that

428
00:19:48,500 --> 00:19:50,000
you're getting to go in it,

429
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:51,500
or to go to war, aren't

430
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:53,000
necessarily people who want to fight,

431
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:54,000
and so they won't be as

432
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:55,000
good soldiers as they will be

433
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:58,000
had they been there because they wanted to be.

434
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
All right, what about Jackie's...

435
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:04,000
Having raised the question of patriotism,

436
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
that patriotism is a better

437
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
or a higher motivation than money

438
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
for military service.

439
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:14,000
Who would like to address that question?

440
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:17,000
Go ahead.

441
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
Patriotism absolutely is not necessary

442
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
in order to be a good soldier,

443
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:24,000
because mercenaries can do just as good of a job

444
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
of the job as anyone

445
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
who waves the American flag around

446
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:31,000
and wants to defend what the

447
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:34,000
government believes that we should do.

448
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:37,000
Did you favor the outsourcing solution?

449
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
Yes, sir.

450
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:45,000
And, all right, so let Jackie respond.

451
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:46,000
What's your name?

452
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:47,000
Philip.

453
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
What about that, Jackie?

454
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
So much for patriotism.

455
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
If you've got someone whose heart

456
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
is in it more than another person's,

457
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
they're going to do a better job.

458
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
When it comes down to the wire and there's, like,

459
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,500
a situation in which someone has

460
00:21:01,500 --> 00:21:04,000
to put their life on the line,

461
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:07,000
someone who's doing it because they love this country

462
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:10,000
will be more willing to go into danger

463
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
than someone who's just getting paid.

464
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:13,000
They don't care.

465
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:14,000
They've got the technical skills,

466
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
but they don't care what happens

467
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:17,000
because they really have...

468
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:20,000
They have nothing, like, nothing invested in this country.

469
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
There's another aspect, though.

470
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:26,000
Once we get onto the issue of patriotism,

471
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:29,000
if you believe patriotism, as Jackie does,

472
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:33,000
should be the foremost consideration and not money,

473
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,500
does that argue for or against

474
00:21:35,500 --> 00:21:38,000
the paid army we have now?

475
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
We call it the volunteer army,

476
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
though if you think about

477
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
it, that's a kind of misnomer.

478
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
A volunteer army, as we use

479
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
the term, is a paid army.

480
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:52,000
So what about the suggestion that patriotism

481
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:56,000
should be the primary motivation for military service,

482
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
not money?

483
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,500
Does that argue in favor of

484
00:22:00,500 --> 00:22:03,000
the paid military that we have,

485
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:07,000
or does it argue for conscription?

486
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
And just to sharpen that point,

487
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:13,000
building on Phil's case for outsourcing,

488
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:18,000
if you think that the all-volunteer army,

489
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:21,000
the paid army, is best

490
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:25,000
because it lets the market allocate positions

491
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:26,000
according to people's needs,

492
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:29,000
people's preferences and willingness to serve

493
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
for a certain wage,

494
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:35,000
doesn't the logic that takes you

495
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
from a system of conscription

496
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:40,000
to the hybrid civil war system,

497
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
to the all-volunteer army,

498
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:47,000
doesn't the idea of expanding freedom of choice

499
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
in the market,

500
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
doesn't that lead you all the way,

501
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:55,000
if you follow that principle consistently,

502
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:58,000
to a more personalized army?

503
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:01,000
And then if you say no,

504
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:02,000
Jackie says no,

505
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:09,000
patriotism should count for something,

506
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:13,000
doesn't that argue for going back to conscription?

507
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:19,000
If by patriotism you mean a sense of civic obligation.

508
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:22,000
Let's see if we can step back

509
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
from the discussion that we've had

510
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:27,000
and see what we've learned

511
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:31,000
about consent

512
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:35,000
as it applies to market exchange.

513
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:42,000
We've really heard two arguments,

514
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:45,000
two arguments against

515
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:50,000
the use of markets and exchange

516
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:53,000
in the allocation of military service.

517
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:59,000
One was the argument raised

518
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:02,000
by Sam and Raul,

519
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:06,000
the argument about coercion.

520
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:10,000
The objection that

521
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:14,000
letting the market allocate military service

522
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
may be unfair

523
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
and may not even be free

524
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:22,000
if there's severe inequality in the society,

525
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:28,000
so that people who buy their way into military service

526
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:35,000
are doing so not because they really want to,

527
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:38,000
but because they have so few economic opportunities

528
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:43,000
that that's their best choice.

529
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:45,500
And Sam and Raul say there's

530
00:24:45,500 --> 00:24:48,000
an element of coercion in that.

531
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
That's one argument.

532
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:53,000
Then there is a second objection

533
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:58,000
to using the market to allocate military service.

534
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
That's the idea

535
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:03,000
that military service shouldn't be treated

536
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:05,000
as just another job for pay

537
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:08,000
because it's bound up with patriotism

538
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
and civic obligation.

539
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
This is a different argument

540
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
from the argument about unfairness

541
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:18,000
and inequality and coercion.

542
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
It's an argument that suggests

543
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:27,000
that maybe where civic obligations are concerned,

544
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:31,000
we shouldn't allocate duties and rights

545
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:35,000
by the market.

546
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:40,000
Now, we've identified two broad objections.

547
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:44,000
What do we need to know to assess those objections?

548
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
To assess the first, the argument

549
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
from coercion, inequality,

550
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:51,000
and unfairness, Sam.

551
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:54,000
We need to ask,

552
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:58,000
what inequalities in the background conditions of society

553
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:03,000
undermine the freedom of choices people make

554
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:07,000
to buy and sell their labor?

555
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,000
Question number one.

556
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:10,000
Question number two,

557
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:15,000
to assess the civic obligation patriotism argument,

558
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:17,000
we have to ask,

559
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:20,000
what are the obligations of citizenship?

560
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:24,000
Is military service one of them or not?

561
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:27,000
What obligates us as citizens?

562
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:30,000
What is the source of political obligation?

563
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:33,000
Is it consent?

564
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:38,000
Or are there some civic obligations we have,

565
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:41,000
even without consent,

566
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:46,000
for living and sharing in a certain kind of society?

567
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:49,000
We haven't answered either of those questions,

568
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:52,000
but our debate today about the Civil War system

569
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:55,000
and the all-volunteer army has at least raised them,

570
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:57,000
and those are questions we're going to return to

571
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
in the coming weeks.

572
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:09,500
Do you think you should be

573
00:27:09,500 --> 00:27:11,000
able to bid for a baby

574
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:13,000
that's up for adoption?

575
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:15,000
That's Andrew's challenge.

576
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:16,500
Do I think I should be

577
00:27:16,500 --> 00:27:18,000
able to bid for a baby?

578
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,000
I'm not sure.

579
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:24,000
It's a market.

580
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:25,000
I mean, I...

581
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:31,000
Today I'd like to turn our attention

582
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:33,000
and get your views

583
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:37,000
about

584
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:40,000
an argument over the role of markets

585
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:44,000
in the realm of human reproduction and procreation.

586
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:51,000
Now, with infertility clinics,

587
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:55,000
people advertise for egg donors.

588
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:59,000
And from time to time in the Harvard Crimson,

589
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:01,000
ads appear for egg donors.

590
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:04,000
Have you seen them?

591
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:08,000
There was one that ran a few years ago

592
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:13,000
that wasn't looking for just any egg donor.

593
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:18,000
It was an ad that offered a large financial incentive

594
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:21,000
for an egg donor from a woman

595
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:24,000
who was intelligent, athletic,

596
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:27,000
at least 5'10",

597
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:35,000
and with at least 1,400 or above on her SATs.

598
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:38,000
How much do you think

599
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:41,000
the person looking for this egg donor

600
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:43,000
was willing to pay?

601
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:45,000
What would you guess?

602
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:53,000
I'll show you the ad.

603
00:28:57,000 --> 00:29:02,000
$50,000 for an egg.

604
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:06,000
But only $100,000.

605
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:08,000
That's a lot of money.

606
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:10,000
That's a lot of money.

607
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
That's a lot of money.

608
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:16,000
But only a premium egg.

609
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:20,000
What do you think about that?

610
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:23,000
Well, there are also sometimes ads

611
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:27,000
in the Harvard Crimson and other college newspapers

612
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:31,000
for sperm donors.

613
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:39,000
So the market in reproductive capacities

614
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:44,000
is an equal opportunity market.

615
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:46,000
Well, not exactly equal opportunity.

616
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:52,000
They're not offering $50,000 for sperm.

617
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:55,000
But there is a company,

618
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:57,000
a large commercial sperm bank,

619
00:29:57,000 --> 00:30:01,000
that markets sperm.

620
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:04,000
It's called California Cryobank.

621
00:30:04,000 --> 00:30:07,000
It's a for-profit company.

622
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:12,000
It imposes exacting standards

623
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:15,000
on the sperm it recruits.

624
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:19,000
And it has offices

625
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:23,000
in Cambridge between Harvard and MIT

626
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:26,000
and in Palo Alto near Stanford.

627
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:33,000
Cryobank's marketing materials

628
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:37,000
play up the prestigious source of its sperm.

629
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:44,000
Here is from the website of Cryobank

630
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:47,000
the information.

631
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:54,000
Here they talk about the compensation.

632
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:57,000
Although compensation should not be

633
00:30:57,000 --> 00:31:00,000
the only reason for becoming a sperm donor,

634
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:03,000
we are aware of the considerable time

635
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:05,000
and expense involved in being a donor.

636
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:14,000
So, do you know what they offer?

637
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:21,000
Donors will be reimbursed $75 per specimen,

638
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:24,000
up to $900 a month

639
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:27,000
if you donate three times a week.

640
00:31:27,000 --> 00:31:28,000
And then they add,

641
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:32,000
we periodically offer incentives such as...

642
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:35,000
Oh.

643
00:31:35,000 --> 00:31:36,000
Oh.

644
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:39,000
Such as movie tickets.

645
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:45,000
Or gift certificates for the extra time and effort

646
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:50,000
expended by participating donors.

647
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:53,000
It's not easy to be a sperm donor.

648
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:59,000
They accept fewer than 5% of the donors who apply.

649
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:03,000
Their admission criteria are more demanding than Harvard's.

650
00:32:03,000 --> 00:32:09,000
The head of the sperm bank said

651
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:12,000
the ideal sperm donor is six feet tall,

652
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:15,000
with a college degree, brown eyes,

653
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:18,000
blonde hair, and dimples.

654
00:32:21,000 --> 00:32:23,000
For the simple reason that these are the traits

655
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:27,000
that the market has shown the customers want.

656
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:30,000
Quote, quoting the head of the sperm bank,

657
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:32,000
if our customers wanted high school dropouts,

658
00:32:32,000 --> 00:32:34,000
we would give them high school dropouts.

659
00:32:37,000 --> 00:32:42,000
So, here are two instances,

660
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:44,000
the market in eggs for donation

661
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:48,000
and the market in sperm,

662
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:50,000
that raise a question.

663
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:55,000
A question about whether eggs and sperm

664
00:32:55,000 --> 00:32:59,000
should or should not be bought and sold for money.

665
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:01,000
As you progress,

666
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:03,000
as you ponder that,

667
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:07,000
I want you to consider another case

668
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:11,000
involving a market

669
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:14,000
and in fact a contract

670
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:17,000
in human reproductive,

671
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:19,000
in the human reproductive capacity.

672
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:21,000
And this is the case

673
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:24,000
of commercial surrogate motherhood.

674
00:33:24,000 --> 00:33:27,000
And it's a case that wound up in court.

675
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:29,000
Some years ago.

676
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:30,000
It's the story of Baby M.

677
00:33:30,000 --> 00:33:35,000
It began with William and Elizabeth Stern,

678
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:38,000
a professional couple wanting a baby,

679
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:40,000
but they couldn't have one on their own,

680
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:44,000
at least not without medical risk to Mrs. Stern.

681
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:47,000
They went to an infertility clinic

682
00:33:47,000 --> 00:33:50,000
where they met Mary Beth Whitehead,

683
00:33:50,000 --> 00:33:52,000
a 29-year-old mother of two,

684
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:55,000
the wife of a sanitation worker.

685
00:33:55,000 --> 00:33:57,000
She had replied to an ad

686
00:33:57,000 --> 00:33:59,000
that the center had placed,

687
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:05,000
seeking the service of a surrogate mother.

688
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:07,000
They made a deal.

689
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:09,000
They signed a contract

690
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:12,000
in which William Stern agreed

691
00:34:12,000 --> 00:34:15,000
to pay Mary Beth Whitehead

692
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:17,000
a $10,000 fee

693
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:20,000
plus all expenses

694
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:22,000
in exchange for which

695
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:24,000
Mary Beth Whitehead agreed

696
00:34:24,000 --> 00:34:26,000
to be artificially inseminated

697
00:34:26,000 --> 00:34:28,000
with William Stern's sperm

698
00:34:28,000 --> 00:34:30,000
to bear the child

699
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:33,000
and then to give the baby

700
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:35,000
to the Sterns.

701
00:34:35,000 --> 00:34:37,000
Well, you probably know

702
00:34:37,000 --> 00:34:39,000
how the story unfolded.

703
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:41,000
Mary Beth gave birth

704
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:43,000
and changed her mind.

705
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:46,000
She decided she wanted to keep the baby.

706
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:49,000
The case wound up in court

707
00:34:49,000 --> 00:34:51,000
in New Jersey.

708
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:53,000
So let's take,

709
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:56,000
put aside any legal questions

710
00:34:56,000 --> 00:34:58,000
and focus on this issue

711
00:34:58,000 --> 00:35:01,000
as a moral question.

712
00:35:01,000 --> 00:35:04,000
How many believe

713
00:35:04,000 --> 00:35:06,000
that the right thing to do

714
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:09,000
in the Baby M case

715
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:11,000
would have been to uphold

716
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:12,000
the contract,

717
00:35:12,000 --> 00:35:15,000
to enforce the contract?

718
00:35:15,000 --> 00:35:17,000
And how many think

719
00:35:17,000 --> 00:35:18,000
the right thing to do

720
00:35:18,000 --> 00:35:20,000
would have been

721
00:35:20,000 --> 00:35:24,000
not to enforce that contract?

722
00:35:24,000 --> 00:35:26,000
So it's about,

723
00:35:26,000 --> 00:35:28,000
the majority say enforce.

724
00:35:28,000 --> 00:35:31,000
So let's now hear the reasons

725
00:35:31,000 --> 00:35:32,000
that people had

726
00:35:32,000 --> 00:35:33,000
either for enforcing

727
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:36,000
or refusing to enforce this contract.

728
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:37,000
First from those,

729
00:35:37,000 --> 00:35:38,000
I want to hear from someone

730
00:35:38,000 --> 00:35:39,000
in the majority.

731
00:35:39,000 --> 00:35:41,000
Why do you uphold the contract?

732
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:43,000
Why do you enforce it?

733
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:45,000
Who can offer a reason?

734
00:35:49,000 --> 00:35:51,000
Yes.

735
00:35:51,000 --> 00:35:53,000
Stand up.

736
00:35:54,000 --> 00:35:59,000
It's a binding contract.

737
00:35:59,000 --> 00:36:01,000
All the parties involved

738
00:36:01,000 --> 00:36:04,000
knew the terms of the contract

739
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:06,000
before any action was taken.

740
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:09,000
It's a voluntary agreement.

741
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:11,000
The mother knew what she was getting into.

742
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:13,000
The, all four,

743
00:36:13,000 --> 00:36:14,000
intelligent adults,

744
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:16,000
regardless of formal education,

745
00:36:16,000 --> 00:36:17,000
whatever.

746
00:36:17,000 --> 00:36:19,000
So it makes sense

747
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:21,000
that if you know what you're getting into beforehand

748
00:36:21,000 --> 00:36:23,000
and you make a promise,

749
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:25,000
you should uphold that promise in the end.

750
00:36:25,000 --> 00:36:26,000
Okay.

751
00:36:26,000 --> 00:36:27,000
A deal is a deal in other words.

752
00:36:27,000 --> 00:36:28,000
Exactly.

753
00:36:28,000 --> 00:36:29,000
And what's your name?

754
00:36:29,000 --> 00:36:30,000
Patrick.

755
00:36:30,000 --> 00:36:31,000
Is Patrick's reason

756
00:36:31,000 --> 00:36:33,000
the reason that most of you

757
00:36:33,000 --> 00:36:34,000
in the majority

758
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:36,000
favored upholding the contract?

759
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:37,000
Yes?

760
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:38,000
All right.

761
00:36:38,000 --> 00:36:40,000
Let's hear now someone

762
00:36:40,000 --> 00:36:43,000
who would not enforce the contract.

763
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:48,000
What do you say to Patrick?

764
00:36:48,000 --> 00:36:50,000
Why not?

765
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:51,000
Yes.

766
00:36:51,000 --> 00:36:52,000
Well, I mean,

767
00:36:52,000 --> 00:36:53,000
I agree.

768
00:36:53,000 --> 00:36:55,000
I think the contract should be upheld

769
00:36:55,000 --> 00:36:57,000
when all the parties know all the information.

770
00:36:57,000 --> 00:36:58,000
But in this case,

771
00:36:58,000 --> 00:37:01,000
I don't think there's a way a mother

772
00:37:01,000 --> 00:37:03,000
before the child exists

773
00:37:03,000 --> 00:37:05,000
could actually know

774
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:07,000
how she's going to feel about that child.

775
00:37:07,000 --> 00:37:09,000
So I don't think the mother

776
00:37:09,000 --> 00:37:11,000
actually had all the information.

777
00:37:11,000 --> 00:37:12,000
She didn't know the person

778
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:13,000
that was going to be born

779
00:37:13,000 --> 00:37:15,000
and didn't know how much she would love that person.

780
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:17,000
So that's my argument.

781
00:37:17,000 --> 00:37:18,000
So you would not,

782
00:37:18,000 --> 00:37:19,000
and what's your name?

783
00:37:19,000 --> 00:37:20,000
Evan Wilson.

784
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:22,000
Evan says he would not uphold the contract

785
00:37:22,000 --> 00:37:26,000
because when it was entered into,

786
00:37:26,000 --> 00:37:30,000
the surrogate mother couldn't

787
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:32,000
be expected to know in advance

788
00:37:32,000 --> 00:37:33,000
how she would feel.

789
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:36,000
So she didn't really have

790
00:37:36,000 --> 00:37:38,000
the relevant information

791
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:41,000
when she made that contract.

792
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:42,000
Who else?

793
00:37:42,000 --> 00:37:48,000
Who else would not uphold the contract?

794
00:37:48,000 --> 00:37:52,000
Yes?

795
00:37:52,000 --> 00:37:53,000
I think,

796
00:37:53,000 --> 00:37:54,000
I also think that a contract

797
00:37:54,000 --> 00:37:55,000
should generally be uphold,

798
00:37:55,000 --> 00:37:57,000
but I think that the child

799
00:37:57,000 --> 00:37:59,000
has an inalienable right

800
00:37:59,000 --> 00:38:03,000
to its actual mother.

801
00:38:03,000 --> 00:38:06,000
And I think that if that mother wants it,

802
00:38:06,000 --> 00:38:07,000
then that child should have

803
00:38:07,000 --> 00:38:09,000
the right to that mother.

804
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:11,000
You mean the biological mother,

805
00:38:11,000 --> 00:38:12,000
not the adoptive mother?

806
00:38:12,000 --> 00:38:13,000
Right.

807
00:38:13,000 --> 00:38:14,000
And why is that?

808
00:38:14,000 --> 00:38:15,000
First of all, tell me your name.

809
00:38:15,000 --> 00:38:16,000
Anna.

810
00:38:16,000 --> 00:38:18,000
Anna. Why is that Anna?

811
00:38:18,000 --> 00:38:20,000
Because I think that

812
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:22,000
that bond that is created

813
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:24,000
by nature is stronger than any bond

814
00:38:24,000 --> 00:38:28,000
that is created by, you know, a contract.

815
00:38:28,000 --> 00:38:30,000
Good. Thank you.

816
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:31,000
Who else?

817
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:32,000
Yes?

818
00:38:32,000 --> 00:38:34,000
I disagree.

819
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:35,000
I don't think that a child has

820
00:38:35,000 --> 00:38:36,000
an inalienable right

821
00:38:36,000 --> 00:38:38,000
to her biological mother.

822
00:38:38,000 --> 00:38:40,000
I think that adoption and surrogacy

823
00:38:40,000 --> 00:38:42,000
are both legitimate trade-offs.

824
00:38:42,000 --> 00:38:45,000
And I agree with the point made

825
00:38:45,000 --> 00:38:48,000
that it's a voluntary agreement.

826
00:38:48,000 --> 00:38:49,000
An individual made it.

827
00:38:49,000 --> 00:38:50,000
It's a voluntary agreement.

828
00:38:50,000 --> 00:38:51,000
And you can't,

829
00:38:51,000 --> 00:38:52,000
okay,

830
00:38:52,000 --> 00:38:54,000
apply coercion to this argument.

831
00:38:54,000 --> 00:38:56,000
You can't apply the objection

832
00:38:56,000 --> 00:38:58,000
from coercion to this argument.

833
00:38:58,000 --> 00:38:59,000
Correct.

834
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:00,000
What's your name?

835
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:01,000
Kathleen.

836
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:03,000
Kathleen, what do you say to Evan

837
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:05,000
that though there may not have been,

838
00:39:05,000 --> 00:39:07,000
Evan claimed that the consent was tainted,

839
00:39:07,000 --> 00:39:08,000
not by coercion,

840
00:39:08,000 --> 00:39:11,000
but by lack of adequate information.

841
00:39:11,000 --> 00:39:13,000
She couldn't have known

842
00:39:13,000 --> 00:39:14,000
the relevant information,

843
00:39:14,000 --> 00:39:17,000
namely how she would feel about the child.

844
00:39:17,000 --> 00:39:18,000
What do you say to that?

845
00:39:18,000 --> 00:39:19,000
I don't think the emotional content

846
00:39:19,000 --> 00:39:21,000
of her feelings plays into this.

847
00:39:21,000 --> 00:39:23,000
I think in a case of law,

848
